Issue #1683 proof: the usage bar divides the counter the gateway enforces

This capture replaces an earlier one taken from the same branch. That one is
superseded rather than lost: it showed bars drawn from the lifetime rollup
while the budget windows behind them were empty, which is exactly the defect
the review caught. The bars in it were pictures of a claim the system was not
making. What follows is the corrected surface, and it deliberately includes a
row in that same state so the fix is visible rather than asserted.

SUBSTRATE

Everything below ran against a stack booted from this branch, on this machine,
with no shared or hosted service involved:

  - a throwaway Postgres (pgvector/pgvector:pg17) carrying the full
    supabase/migrations chain, plus GoTrue and PostgREST behind one nginx
    origin, all stood up by scripts/ci-supabase-stack.sh, the same script the
    CI web-e2e job uses. It was destroyed after the capture.
  - control-plane BUILT FROM THIS BRANCH (docker compose --build), so the
    budget_spend_credits field on the list response is this diff's code and not
    a stub, running against that database and healthy on /health.
  - a production `next build` plus `next start` of apps/web-console from this
    branch, pointed at that control-plane, driven by a real Chromium through
    Playwright.

The three fixture users came from
apps/web-console/tests/e2e/support/e2e-auth-fixtures.mjs with a run-scoped
E2E_RUN_KEY, so no shared fixture account was touched and no password anywhere
was reset or rotated. The browser signed in through the console's own
/auth/sign-in form against that throwaway GoTrue.

The five API keys were created through the real product API, not by writing
rows: POST /api/v1/accounts/current/api-keys for each key and POST
/api/v1/accounts/current/api-keys/{id}/policy for each cap, both carrying the
signed-in user's bearer.

The counters were written directly, one INSERT per row, because producing them
through the gateway would mean paying a provider for tokens. Both tables were
written, and which row went where is the substance of this capture:

  - public.api_key_usage_rollups, the table RecordUsageFinalization writes on
    every settled request and Repository.GetLifetimeSpend sums.
  - public.api_key_budget_windows, the table ApplyReservationDelta writes only
    once a cap exists and GetBudgetWindow reads, which is what edge-api's
    authz.CheckAccess compares against (consumed + reserved + estimated >
    limit).

The key named capped-after-spending has a rollup row and NO window row. That is
not an oversight in the fixture; it is the reachable state a key is in when it
spent while uncapped and was capped afterwards, and it is the state that used
to render as a full red bar reading 148.5 percent with a "Limit reached" badge
while the gateway would still have served it.

WHAT THE DATABASE HOLDS

public.api_key_budget_windows, nickname / window kind / consumed / reserved:

  batch-jobs    lifetime  2970000000  0
  nightly-sync  monthly   2500000000  0
  prod-gateway  lifetime  1140000000  0

capped-after-spending and playground have no row at all in that table.

WHAT THE CONTROL-PLANE SERVED

GET /api/v1/accounts/current/api-keys, read back before the browser ran:

  playground             spend=   720000000  budget_spend=  null  limit=  null         kind=none
  capped-after-spending  spend=  2970000000  budget_spend=     0  limit=  2000000000   kind=lifetime
  nightly-sync           spend= 12000000000  budget_spend=  2500000000  limit= 10000000000  kind=monthly
  batch-jobs             spend=  2970000000  budget_spend=  2970000000  limit=  2000000000   kind=lifetime
  prod-gateway           spend=  1140000000  budget_spend=  1140000000  limit=  5000000000   kind=lifetime

budget_spend_credits tracks the window table above, row for row, including the
zero for the key whose spend predates its cap. One US dollar is one billion
credits.

SCREENSHOTS

URL for all three: http://localhost:3010/console/api-keys
No credential appears in that URL, in any capture, or in this log. There is no
address bar in the captures.

01-api-keys-usage-bars.png
  The whole page after signing in. Five rows, five states of the same column.

02-usage-bars-closeup.png
  The table alone, which is where the change is legible:

    - prod-gateway: "$1.14 of $5.00 total", "22.8%", a bar filled about a fifth
      of its track in the accent colour. Window and rollup agree, so no
      lifetime figure is printed beneath.
    - batch-jobs: "$2.97 of $2.00 total", a danger-toned "Limit reached" badge,
      "148.5%", and a red bar that stops exactly at the end of its track. The
      window really does hold 2.97 of a 2.00 cap, so the gateway really would
      refuse this key's next request.
    - nightly-sync: "$2.50 of $10.00/mo", "25.0%", a quarter-filled bar, and
      "$12.00 lifetime" beneath it. The bar divides this month's window, which
      is the window the monthly cap is enforced on; the lifetime total is a
      different fact and is printed as its own figure rather than joined to the
      cap by a connective.
    - capped-after-spending: "$0 of $2.00 total", "0.0%", an empty bar, no
      badge, and "$2.97 lifetime" beneath it. This is the row that carries the
      fix. Before it, this key rendered at 148.5 percent in red.
    - playground: "$0.72 of Unlimited" and no bar, because there is no
      denominator. The lifetime figure is the only spend an uncapped key has:
      its budget window is never written at all.

03-usage-bars-dark.png
  The same table with the dark colour scheme emulated, confirming the track,
  the accent fill and the danger fill all still read against the dark surface.

WHAT THE PROGRESS ELEMENTS ACTUALLY CARRY

Read out of the live DOM in the same browser session, in row order:

  capped-after-spending  value="0"    max="100"
                         aria-label="Budget used: $0 of $2.00 total"
                         aria-valuetext="0.0%"
  nightly-sync           value="25"   max="100"
                         aria-label="Budget used: $2.50 of $10.00/mo"
                         aria-valuetext="25.0%"
  batch-jobs             value="100"  max="100"
                         aria-label="Budget used: $2.97 of $2.00 total"
                         aria-valuetext="148.5%, limit reached"
  prod-gateway           value="22.8" max="100"
                         aria-label="Budget used: $1.14 of $5.00 total"
                         aria-valuetext="22.8%"

Four of them, one per capped key with an enforced counter, and none for the
uncapped row. They are native <progress> elements, so the progressbar role, the
value and the maximum are the element's own; the accessible name carries both
dollar figures, and aria-valuetext exists so a screen reader hears "148.5%" for
the over-limit key rather than the "100%" its clamped fill would otherwise
announce. Because support for that attribute is not uniform, the true
percentage is also a text node in the same cell.

Full row text as rendered, tabs and newlines flattened:

  playground             | hk_xxxx(redacted) Active | $0.72 of Unlimited
  capped-after-spending  | hk_xxxx(redacted) Active | $0 of $2.00 total 0.0%
                           $2.97 lifetime
  nightly-sync           | hk_xxxx(redacted) Active | $2.50 of $10.00/mo 25.0%
                           $12.00 lifetime
  batch-jobs             | hk_xxxx(redacted) Active | $2.97 of $2.00 total
                           Limit reached 148.5%
  prod-gateway           | hk_xxxx(redacted) Active | $1.14 of $5.00 total 22.8%

The key suffixes the product itself redacts down to four characters are shown
as (redacted) here as well. They belonged to keys in a database that no longer
exists.

BROWSER CONSOLE

No console errors or warnings were emitted during the capture.
